{"slug":"/students/subjects/chemistry/states-of-matter/gas-laws-boyle-charles/","source":"students/subjects/chemistry/states-of-matter/gas-laws-boyle-charles.html","area":"students","kind":"content","title":"Gas Laws | Chemistry","description":"","subject":"chemistry","body":"<div class=\"max-w-3xl mx-auto\">\n    <nav class=\"mb-4\"><a href=\"/students/subjects/chemistry/states-of-matter/\" class=\"text-blue-600\">← Back to States of Matter</a></nav>\n    <article class=\"content-card rounded-lg p-6\">\n      <h1 class=\"text-xl font-bold mb-2\">Gas Laws (Boyle &amp; Charles)</h1>\n      <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">Statements and simple worked examples: Boyle's law (P ∝ 1/V) and Charles's law (V ∝ T).</p>\n\n      <h3 class=\"mt-4 font-semibold\">Worked example</h3>\n      <p class=\"text-gray-700\">If 2.0 L of gas at 1.0 atm is compressed to 0.5 L at constant temperature, what is the new pressure? Using P1V1=P2V2 → P2= P1V1/V2 = 1.0×2.0/0.5 = 4.0 atm.</p>\n    </article>\n  </div>\n<!-- Topic Quiz System -->","bodyClass":"min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-cyan-50 to-sky-100 p-6","bodyStyle":"","css":["body{font-family:Inter, sans-serif}.content-card{-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(12px);backdrop-filter:blur(12px);background:rgba(255,255,255,0.95);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2);}"],"quiz":null,"tracker":null,"links":[]}